        {
            "number": 6,
            "revision": "44c209d712a19884fcb30d24049c51dfd8f5b05d",
            "parents": [
                "02c4366be0eb2409c81784c989dfb690cd41af58"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/26/77426/6",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "PeterYinGit",
                "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                "username": "PeterYinGit"
            },
            "createdOn": 1747752810,
            "author": {
                "name": "PeterYinGit",
                "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                "username": "PeterYinGit"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "src/psu/PSUSensorMain.cpp",
                    "line": 497,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "ChauLy-Vietnam",
                        "email": "chaul@amperecomputing.com",
                        "username": "chaul-ampere"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't think we should change this boolean, which represents that this device was probed by instantiateDevices(). But hasLabelPowerState() should be a condition next to `if (activateOnly && !findI2CDev->second.second)`, like `if (activateOnly && (!findI2CDev->second.second || hasLabelPowerState(*baseConfig)))`. That means the device was already probed before and the sensor was there, but needs to be re-activated.\n\nBut I wonder why this condition passes`https://github.com/openbmc/dbus-sensors/blob/02c4366be0eb2409c81784c989dfb690cd41af58/src/DeviceMgmt.hpp#L138` when the deactived sensor has `isActive() == false`. Otherwise, the device will be instantiated again and has `findI2CDev->second.second == true` => then be able to pass your condition."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/psu/PSUSensorMain.cpp",
                    "line": 497,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Mikhail Zhvakin",
                        "email": "striker_1993@mail.ru",
                        "username": "striman"
                    },
                    "message": "Faced the same problem. First time seeing this code. Here are my thoughts.\n\n`findI2CDev->second.second` is about I2C device.\n`isActive()` is about sensor.\n\nOne I2C device can provide multiple sensors. And for example only one of them can be deactivated.\n\n`https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/dbus-sensors/+/77426/comment/e7c66643_b1907129/`\nI wonder why they only check the first sensor found if it is active or not. It looks wrong.\n\n\nDo they want to reinstantiate the I2C device if one of the sensors is deactivated? I don't know."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/psu/PSUSensorMain.cpp",
                    "line": 497,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "ChauLy-Vietnam",
                        "email": "chaul@amperecomputing.com",
                        "username": "chaul-ampere"
                    },
                    "message": "> I wonder why they only check the first sensor found if it is active or not. It looks wrong.\n\nYeah, agree.\n\n> Do they want to reinstantiate the I2C device if one of the sensors is deactivated? I don't know.\n\nI think it instead should be: if there's one active sensor found that belongs to the device, the device info should be enlisted from that sensor.\n\n`createSensorsCallback` should check for sensor's active state to return instead of device's. But I think the Label power state check is acceptable."
                }
            ],
